James wanted to generalize his `dplyr` code that uses `mutate()` and `stats::filter()` for calculations involving persistence levels. I provided a function, `persistent_sum`, to encapsulate these operations, allowing for cleaner code while discussing his coding style preferences. He prefers compact functions without trailing parentheses, fewer lines per argument, and no spaces around equal signs in default arguments. We also clarified the necessity of using `as.numeric()` for stripping time-series attributes and discussed alternatives for handling `persist` values. Finally, James expressed a preference for using dots instead of underscores in function and variable names.
